{% doc %}
  Renders a product rating from Shopify's standard rating metafield.

  Reads `product.metafields.reviews.rating` and `rating_count`, which is the
  shape every major review app writes to. Nothing is fetched and nothing is
  stored: a theme that collected its own reviews would be doing an app's job,
  which the Theme Store rejects.

  The stars are decorative. The rating is announced as a translated sentence
  through visually hidden text, because five star glyphs read aloud are five
  identical words with no number attached.

  Renders nothing when the metafield is missing, so a store without a review app
  gets no empty row rather than "0 out of 5".

  ## Driving it without a product

  `value` and `count` override the metafield, which is what an onboarding
  placeholder needs: a card with no product behind it still has to show the row
  of stars, or the empty state is a different shape from the thing it stands in
  for. Passing them renders exactly the same markup from the same loop, so the
  placeholder cannot drift from the real card — which a second hand-written row
  of star icons would, the first time the scale or the class names changed.

  @param {product} [product]
  @param {boolean} [show_count] - Appends the review count. Default true
  @param {number} [value] - Rating out of `max`. Overrides the metafield.
  @param {number} [count] - Review count. Overrides the metafield.
  @param {number} [max] - Scale maximum when `value` is passed. Default 5.
  @param {string} [class]

  @example
    {% if settings.show_rating %}
      {% render 'rating', product: product %}
    {% endif %}
{% enddoc %}

{%- liquid
  assign show_the_count = true
  if show_count == false
    assign show_the_count = false
  endif

  comment
    Two sources, one shape. `value` passed in wins outright — it is the caller
    saying there is no metafield to read and here is what to draw instead — and
    everything below this point works from `star_value`, `star_count` and
    `scale_max` without knowing which of the two filled them.
  endcomment
  assign has_rating = false

  if value != blank
    assign star_value = value | times: 1.0
    assign star_count = count | default: 0 | plus: 0
    assign scale_max = max | default: 5 | plus: 0
    assign has_rating = true
  else
    assign rating = product.metafields.reviews.rating.value
    assign star_count = product.metafields.reviews.rating_count.value | default: 0
    if rating != blank and rating.rating != blank and star_count > 0
      assign scale_max = rating.scale_max | default: 5
      assign star_value = rating.rating | times: 1.0
      assign has_rating = true
    endif
  endif
-%}

{%- if has_rating -%}
  {%- liquid
    assign value = star_value
    assign count = star_count
    assign rounded = star_value | round: 1
  -%}

  <div class="rating {{ class }}" data-rating="{{ rounded }}">
    <div class="rating__stars" aria-hidden="true">
      {%- for index in (1..scale_max) -%}
        {%- liquid
          assign threshold = index | minus: 0.5
        -%}
        {%- if value >= index -%}
          {% render 'icon', icon: 'star', class: 'rating__star rating__star--full' %}
        {%- elsif value >= threshold -%}
          {% render 'icon', icon: 'star-half', class: 'rating__star rating__star--half' %}
        {%- else -%}
          {% render 'icon', icon: 'star', class: 'rating__star rating__star--empty' %}
        {%- endif -%}
      {%- endfor -%}
    </div>

    <span class="visually-hidden">
      {{- 'products.product.rating_html' | t: rating: rounded, max: scale_max -}}
    </span>

    {%- if show_the_count -%}
      <span class="rating__count" aria-hidden="true">({{ count }})</span>
      <span class="visually-hidden">{{ 'products.product.rating_count' | t: count: count }}</span>
    {%- endif -%}
  </div>
{%- endif -%}
